  "source": {
    "citation": "Kaminsky LA, Arena R, Myers J. Reference Standards for Cardiorespiratory Fitness (FRIEND registry). Mayo Clin Proc. 2015;90(11):1515-1523, as tabulated in ACSM's Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription, 10th ed. Distance conversion: Cooper KH. JAMA. 1968;203(3):201-204.",
    "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2015.07.026",
    "population": "US adults 20-79 who underwent maximal treadmill cardiopulmonary exercise testing. A referred, self-selected group, fitter than the general public.",
    "provisional": true,
    "transcription_verified": false,
    "notes": "PROVISIONAL because of the conversion chain, not the source. Published VO2max percentiles are converted to 12-minute run distance with the 1968 Cooper regression, then to a mile time assuming a mile is run about 5 per cent quicker than 12-minute pace. Each step is a documented equation and each adds error; the Cooper regression alone carries roughly a 250m individual standard error. Percentile ORDER survives all of it because every step is monotone, so your ranking is sounder than the absolute time implies. Decade tables are interpolated to 5-year bands."
  },
